Z||000005||theogre||04-20-99||07:49 PM||||the fuel pump does draw a good amount of juice. So does the electric fan, for the gigles of it, lift the front lid and make sure it's stopped. ((Acoustics are a funny thing, just cause it sounds like it coming from a particular place doesn't make it so.))<p>The fuel pump relay may have fried, or if I'm reading this right, there is also an oil presure switch in that circuit. It looks like either one could cause the fuel pump to stay on. The ECM switch, and relay control the pump durring starting, but once oil presure is up, the relay is over ridden. This type thing is usually done to prevent relay coil burn out. there is the remote posiblity that the ECM lost it, but replace the relay, and oil switch. they are cheap compaired to the ECM.
